// Making a request while the program runs, and the vocabulary of the URL it is
// made to.
//
//	match var r = http.get(url="{server}/api/v2/pokemon/", query={limit=20, offset=offset}) {
//	    ok { … r.status, r.body … }
//	    failed { … }   // r.kind is transport or timeout
//	}
//
// `send` answers an `async.Result`, and a failure only where no response
// arrived -- `transport` or `timeout`. A 404 is a response: its status is read
// off it, and `Response.checked` turns a status that is no success into the
// failure it says, so a caller that wants only a 2xx writes
// `http.get(…)` then `r.checked()`.
//
// **A query is a struct**, a field per parameter, as a translation's arguments
// are: an absent option is left off the URL, a list repeats its key, and
// anything else is written as `string(v)` writes it. The key is the field's
// name, or `#[http.identifier("page-size")]` where the parameter's name is no
// SNGL identifier -- a struct declared for the purpose, since an anonymous
// literal has no field to carry the mark. A field holding a struct, a map or
// anything without a string form has no place in a URL, and is refused at the
// call.
//
// A call waits, as any async function does: Go makes it off the thread the
// target draws on, JavaScript awaits a `fetch` and Kotlin suspends on a
// coroutine. bubbletea answers no `async.do` yet, so a request there is refused
// at the call that waits.
//
// Not yet: a header or cookie parameter beyond the `headers` map, a body
// beyond a string, streaming, a timeout setting and credentials.
